#{[17592186562015 :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562029 :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186562034 :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562037 :answer/value "$\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186562040 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562066 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562073 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562080 :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562095 :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562099 :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562100 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562107 :answer/value "$\\lambda =7\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186562112 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562128 :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562143 :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562159 :answer/value "$\\lambda =-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 6\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186562173 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562191 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562196 :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562213 :answer/value "$\\lambda =4\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186562214 :answer/value "$\\lambda =-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186562397 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ -6+\\text{i} \\\\ 5+\\text{i} \\\\ 3-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+5\\text{i} \\\\ -4-4\\text{i} \\\\ 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$"] [17592186562397 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{array}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 1 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562405 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$"] [17592186562405 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )^{2}-5(-5)) & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +26) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 26}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562410 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$"] [17592186562410 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-2(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +20) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{(-8)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 20}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562414 :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R))=3\\cdot 9=27.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"] [17592186562429 :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R))=4\\cdot 9=36.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"] [17592186562432 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -13 \\\\ -6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$"] [17592186562432 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 0 & -13 \\\\ -6 & -7-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-7-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-2(-13)) & =0 \\\\ (-7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +34) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 34}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562433 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,$ $n=2,$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nSince $n=2,$ we need to compute the first $2-1=1$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\ 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186562436 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{2}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(2)\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(2),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(2),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ \\since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186562448 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$"] [17592186562448 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 5\\end{array}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{1,2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562450 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$"] [17592186562450 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 13 & -5-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(-5-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$"] [17592186562451 :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{7}(R))={7}^{2}=49.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"] [17592186562465 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$"] [17592186562465 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=3.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i}=-1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562468 :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nThus, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R)$ contains $5\\cdot 1=5$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R),$ we have, indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5\\times 1}(R))=5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"] [17592186562471 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{6} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5\\pi }{6} & \\pi \\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\pi \\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ find $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡E.$"] [17592186562471 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡E$ and the given matrix $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡E=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡D=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡E & =P\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186562497 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5-\\text{i} \\\\ -2+5\\text{i} \\\\ -4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4+4\\text{i} \\\\ 1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 4-6\\text{i} \\\\ 1-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-4-\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$"] [17592186562497 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{array}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562503 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-xy)=2-4x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4(0.5)-(1) & -(0.5) \\\\ 4(1) & -2+4(0.5)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"] [17592186562510 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$"] [17592186562510 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-7-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((-7-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-5(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +41) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{(10)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 41}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562511 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$"] [17592186562511 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4+(-2)\\text{i}=4-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -4∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186562516 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3+5\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$"] [17592186562516 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186562518 :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3-2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$"] [17592186562518 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186562970 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562982 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562988 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186562996 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ e\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563026 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}"] [17592186563032 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563048 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563083 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563103 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563112 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563132 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563143 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563174 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563210 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563214 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563229 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563330 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563333 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563350 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563351 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563408 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563411 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563456 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563484 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563509 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ e \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563648 :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\sqrt{t} \\\\ t+3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$  is continuous."] [17592186563656 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0) & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $x-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186563663 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563663 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+4{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563664 :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563664 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1}\\right) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{′}}{(t-1)^{′}}\\right) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of ${lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+0\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563667 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ (1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563667 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & ={lim}_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563678 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563678 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{array}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-6\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-6.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-6\\,⇒\\,k=6.\n$$"] [17592186563693 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563693 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563702 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186563702 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1))\\, \\\\ \\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563712 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563712 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563716 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$"] [17592186563716 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563719 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186563719 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{array}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-1)=-2.$"] [17592186563727 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
